1
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:07,704
A team packages its static site with Nginx.

2
00:00:07,704 --> 00:00:11,891
docker run -d prints a container ID, so everything looks fine,

3
00:00:11,891 --> 00:00:17,700
but the site is not reachable and the container is gone from docker ps a second later.

4
00:00:18,233 --> 00:00:18,370
1.

5
00:00:18,370 --> 00:00:21,588
What did the container print before it stopped?

6
00:00:22,133 --> 00:00:24,836
CMD ["nginx"] starts Nginx as a daemon.

7
00:00:24,836 --> 00:00:30,035
The foreground process returns as soon as Nginx has gone to the background,

8
00:00:30,035 --> 00:00:35,718
so the container's main process ends with status 0 and Docker stops the container.

9
00:00:37,467 --> 00:00:40,012
Run the server in the foreground.

10
00:00:40,012 --> 00:00:43,561
The fixed Dockerfile keeps Nginx as process 1:

11
00:00:44,100 --> 00:00:48,426
Every container's main command must run in the foreground:

12
00:00:48,426 --> 00:00:51,485
nginx -g 'daemon off;', gunicorn without.

13
00:00:52,033 --> 00:00:59,723
Start from the official image's default CMD unless you have a reason not to (the nginx image already sets.

14
00:01:01,467 --> 00:01:04,883
A new image of the orders service never starts.

15
00:01:04,883 --> 00:01:11,351
docker run fails at once, and the container appears in docker ps -a as Created, never Up.

16
00:01:11,900 --> 00:01:12,065
1.

17
00:01:12,065 --> 00:01:14,370
Read the error from the end.

18
00:01:14,933 --> 00:01:17,242
A typo in the Dockerfile:

19
00:01:17,242 --> 00:01:19,552
CMD ["pyhton", "app.py"].

20
00:01:19,552 --> 00:01:23,893
The program pyhton does not exist in the image.

21
00:01:27,133 --> 00:01:31,509
Run the image once locally (or in CI) before pushing it:

22
00:01:31,509 --> 00:01:35,103
docker run --rm IMAGE catches this in seconds.

23
00:01:35,667 --> 00:01:39,950
Use the exec form (CMD ["python", "app.py"]):

24
00:01:39,950 --> 00:01:46,136
the error is explicit and signals reach the process (lesson 027).

25
00:01:47,900 --> 00:01:54,132
Version 2.0 of the price list was built and "deployed", yet users still see version 1.0.

26
00:01:54,132 --> 00:01:59,160
The container runs, the logs are clean, and there is no error anywhere:

27
00:01:59,160 --> 00:02:03,905
the container simply runs a different image than everyone believes.

28
00:02:04,467 --> 00:02:04,613
1.

29
00:02:04,613 --> 00:02:07,182
Which image does the container run?

30
00:02:07,182 --> 00:02:12,391
docker ps shows the name it was started with, not what that name meant:

31
00:02:12,933 --> 00:02:20,158
The deployment referenced price-list (implicitly :latest), which still pointed to the 1.0 build.

32
00:02:20,158 --> 00:02:22,491
Nothing was broken technically:

33
00:02:22,491 --> 00:02:24,523
the wrong image was chosen.

34
00:02:26,267 --> 00:02:29,036
Deploy an explicit version tag:

35
00:02:29,600 --> 00:02:31,680
Never deploy latest.

36
00:02:32,233 --> 00:02:40,888
Write the version and the Git commit into labels at build time (LABEL org.opencontainers.image.version=…) and.

37
00:02:42,633 --> 00:02:47,185
A developer starts the new version of the website next to the old one,

38
00:02:47,185 --> 00:02:50,697
and docker run refuses with port is already allocated.

39
00:02:50,697 --> 00:02:53,558
The new container exists but is not running.

40
00:02:54,100 --> 00:02:54,264
1.

41
00:02:54,264 --> 00:02:56,800
Which container holds the port?

42
00:02:57,333 --> 00:03:01,396
Two containers asked for the same host port 8080.

43
00:03:01,396 --> 00:03:04,298
The old one still runs and owns it.

44
00:03:06,033 --> 00:03:06,777
Pick one:

45
00:03:06,777 --> 00:03:12,393
stop the old container first, or give the new one another host port.

46
00:03:12,393 --> 00:03:14,623
Here both run side by side:

47
00:03:15,167 --> 00:03:27,856
Keep a port plan for local development (for example 8080 web, 8081 API, 5432 database) and write it into the.

48
00:03:28,400 --> 00:03:36,284
Remove containers you no longer need (docker rm -f, docker compose down) instead of leaving them running.

49
00:03:38,033 --> 00:03:42,516
The container is Up, the logs show the server started,

50
00:03:42,516 --> 00:03:47,913
but the browser and curl cannot connect to http://localhost:8080.

51
00:03:48,467 --> 00:03:50,363
Work from the inside out:

52
00:03:50,363 --> 00:03:56,812
is the application running, does it answer inside the container, is a port published?

53
00:03:56,812 --> 00:03:56,964
1.

54
00:03:56,964 --> 00:03:58,026
Is it running?

55
00:03:58,567 --> 00:04:01,191
The container was started without -p:

56
00:04:01,191 --> 00:04:04,241
no host port forwards to container port 80.

57
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:11,746
Port mappings are set when a container is created; they cannot be added later.

58
00:04:11,746 --> 00:04:13,219
Recreate it with -p:

59
00:04:13,767 --> 00:04:20,706
Define published ports in a Compose file instead of remembering -p flags (lesson 064).

60
00:04:21,267 --> 00:04:30,631
After starting a service, check docker ps for the 0.0.0.0:…->… mapping, not only for Up.

61
00:04:32,367 --> 00:04:37,363
The tickets service works on the developer's laptop with python app.py.

62
00:04:37,363 --> 00:04:44,751
In a container, with the port published correctly, every request from the host fails with an empty reply.

63
00:04:45,300 --> 00:04:45,485
1.

64
00:04:45,485 --> 00:04:47,515
Running and published?

65
00:04:48,067 --> 00:04:50,978
The application binds to 127.0.0.1.

66
00:04:50,978 --> 00:04:56,718
That is fine on a laptop, where the browser runs on the same machine,

67
00:04:56,718 --> 00:05:01,626
but inside a container "localhost" is the container itself.

68
00:05:03,367 --> 00:05:07,191
Bind to 0.0.0.0 (all interfaces).

69
00:05:07,191 --> 00:05:15,070
Many frameworks have the same default and a flag or variable for it:

70
00:05:15,070 --> 00:05:27,816
Flask --host 0.0.0.0, uvicorn --host 0.0.0.0, Node server.listen(port, '0.0.0.0'), Spring Boot server.address.

71
00:05:28,367 --> 00:05:39,856
Make the listen address configurable and default it to 0.0.0.0 in the image (for example ENV HOST=0.0.0.0).

72
00:05:40,400 --> 00:05:54,400
Publish to 127.0.0.1 on the host side (-p 127.0.0.1:8080:8080) when you want to restrict access, never by.

73
00:05:56,133 --> 00:06:03,112
The team's services listen on port 8080, so a developer starts Nginx the same way:

74
00:06:03,112 --> 00:06:04,219
-p 8080:8080.

75
00:06:04,219 --> 00:06:11,198
The container is Up, the port is published, and every request gets an empty reply.

76
00:06:11,733 --> 00:06:11,891
1.

77
00:06:11,891 --> 00:06:14,893
Which port does the image say it uses?

78
00:06:15,433 --> 00:06:24,043
-p 8080:8080 maps to container port 8080; the application listens on container port 80.

79
00:06:27,300 --> 00:06:33,675
Read EXPOSE (or the image's documentation) before choosing the container side of -p.

80
00:06:34,233 --> 00:06:36,294
Remember the order:

81
00:06:36,294 --> 00:06:42,478
-p HOST:CONTAINER, as in -p 8080:80 = "my 8080 → its 80".

82
00:06:44,233 --> 00:06:47,234
The application was split into two networks:

83
00:06:47,234 --> 00:06:51,189
frontend for the public side, backend for the data stores.

84
00:06:51,189 --> 00:06:57,803
Since then the API cannot reach its Redis cache by name, although the container cache is running.

85
00:06:58,367 --> 00:06:58,578
1.

86
00:06:58,578 --> 00:07:00,686
Both containers run?

87
00:07:01,233 --> 00:07:05,662
The API needs both networks (public traffic on frontend,

88
00:07:05,662 --> 00:07:10,248
data stores on backend) but was attached to frontend only.

89
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:16,885
Connect the running container to the second network (no restart needed):

90
00:07:17,433 --> 00:07:25,003
Declare networks per service in Compose, so membership is reviewed in code (lesson 065):

91
00:07:25,567 --> 00:07:31,776
Keep data stores on an internal network only, and attach exactly the services that need them.

92
00:07:33,533 --> 00:07:38,038
Two containers started with plain docker run, no network options.

93
00:07:38,038 --> 00:07:46,078
The API cannot reach cache by name, but a colleague notices that the same command with the cache's IP address works.

94
00:07:46,633 --> 00:07:46,890
1.

95
00:07:46,890 --> 00:07:48,558
Same network?

96
00:07:49,100 --> 00:07:55,389
Both containers are on the default bridge network, where Docker does not resolve container names.

97
00:07:57,133 --> 00:08:00,808
Use a user-defined network for every application:

98
00:08:01,367 --> 00:08:05,137
Never rely on the default bridge for applications:

99
00:08:05,137 --> 00:08:09,436
create a network (docker network create) or use Compose,.

100
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:14,350
Refer to services by name, never by IP address.

101
00:08:16,100 --> 00:08:20,988
The API was moved into a container next to its PostgreSQL container.

102
00:08:20,988 --> 00:08:27,456
It worked on the laptop, where PostgreSQL ran locally; in the container it cannot connect:

103
00:08:27,456 --> 00:08:31,985
Connection refused, although the database container is running.

104
00:08:32,533 --> 00:08:32,690
1.

105
00:08:32,690 --> 00:08:35,283
Is the database up and listening?

106
00:08:35,833 --> 00:08:40,448
The connection setting PGHOST=localhost was copied from the laptop.

107
00:08:40,448 --> 00:08:46,026
In a container it points to the client itself; the database is another container,

108
00:08:46,026 --> 00:08:49,263
reachable by its name db on the shared network.

109
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:57,164
Configure the host by service name, through the environment (lesson 059):

110
00:08:57,700 --> 00:09:01,456
Never hard-code localhost for another service:

111
00:09:01,456 --> 00:09:05,865
read DB_HOST (or a DATABASE_URL) from the environment.

112
00:09:06,400 --> 00:09:10,181
Wait for readiness, not for "container started":

113
00:09:10,181 --> 00:09:12,860
a healthcheck with pg_isready and.

114
00:09:14,600 --> 00:09:21,194
The billing service starts on a developer's machine but stops immediately on the test server with exit code 1.

115
00:09:21,194 --> 00:09:26,590
The deployment script was copied from another service and does not pass any configuration.

116
00:09:27,133 --> 00:09:27,336
1.

117
00:09:27,336 --> 00:09:29,158
What did it print?

118
00:09:29,700 --> 00:09:33,683
The container was started without the required DATABASE_URL.

119
00:09:33,683 --> 00:09:37,932
Environment variables are not inherited from the server's shell:

120
00:09:37,932 --> 00:09:43,310
each container gets only what -e, --env-file, Compose or the image's ENV give it.

121
00:09:45,067 --> 00:09:51,056
Pass the configuration from a file kept next to the deployment (lesson 060):

122
00:09:51,600 --> 00:09:59,785
Fail at start-up with a clear message when a required variable is missing (as app.py does), never later.

123
00:10:00,333 --> 00:10:08,063
Document every variable in a committed .env.example; keep the real .env out of Git and out of the image.

124
00:10:09,800 --> 00:10:17,283
A security review asked the team to run the reports service as a non-root user and to "make the settings file private".

125
00:10:17,283 --> 00:10:21,496
After the change, the container stops at once with PermissionError:

126
00:10:21,496 --> 00:10:23,320
[Errno 13] Permission denied.

127
00:10:23,867 --> 00:10:24,064
1.

128
00:10:24,064 --> 00:10:26,136
Who runs the process?

129
00:10:26,700 --> 00:10:33,635
chmod 600 made the file readable by root only, while the container runs as the non-root user app.

130
00:10:35,400 --> 00:10:39,850
Give the application user exactly the access it needs, no more:

131
00:10:39,850 --> 00:10:42,745
owned by root (the app cannot change it),

132
00:10:42,745 --> 00:10:48,255
readable by the app group, with COPY --chown and --chmod (no extra RUN layer):

133
00:10:48,800 --> 00:10:51,758
Decide ownership in the Dockerfile:

134
00:10:51,758 --> 00:10:54,885
COPY --chown=USER:GROUP --chmod=MODE.

135
00:10:55,433 --> 00:10:57,661
Test the image as it will run:

136
00:10:57,661 --> 00:11:02,858
docker run --rm IMAGE id and ls -l in CI catch this before deployment.

137
00:11:04,600 --> 00:11:08,237
The uploads service must write to its data/ directory.

138
00:11:08,237 --> 00:11:12,008
After moving it to a non-root user it fails at start-up,

139
00:11:12,008 --> 00:11:19,080
and after the next hardening step (a read-only root file system) it fails again with a different message.

140
00:11:19,633 --> 00:11:19,795
1.

141
00:11:19,795 --> 00:11:23,038
Who runs it, and who owns the directory?

142
00:11:23,600 --> 00:11:23,753
1.

143
00:11:23,753 --> 00:11:29,562
data/ was created by root during the build, and the application runs as app.

144
00:11:29,562 --> 00:11:29,715
2.

145
00:11:29,715 --> 00:11:36,900
With --read-only, every path that must be written needs its own writable mount; /tmp had none.

146
00:11:38,633 --> 00:11:43,877
The fixed image gives data/ to the app user and declares it as a volume.

147
00:11:43,877 --> 00:11:50,141
At run time, keep the root file system read-only and mount exactly the writable paths:

148
00:11:50,141 --> 00:11:54,293
a named volume for the data, a tmpfs for temporary files:

149
00:11:54,833 --> 00:12:02,128
In the Dockerfile, create every directory the app writes to and chown it to the app user; leave the rest owned.

150
00:12:02,667 --> 00:12:09,121
List the writable paths in the image documentation and in Compose (read_only:

151
00:12:09,121 --> 00:12:10,127
true, tmpfs:

152
00:12:10,127 --> 00:12:11,636
[/tmp], volumes:).

153
00:12:13,400 --> 00:12:17,652
The notes service was given a named volume "so the notes survive".

154
00:12:17,652 --> 00:12:24,030
After the container was recreated, the notes are gone anyway, and the volume turns out to be empty.

155
00:12:24,567 --> 00:12:24,731
1.

156
00:12:24,731 --> 00:12:27,036
Where is the volume mounted?

157
00:12:27,600 --> 00:12:31,305
A typo in the mount target (/dat instead of /data):

158
00:12:31,305 --> 00:12:35,520
the volume and the application's data directory never met.

159
00:12:38,767 --> 00:12:40,788
Declare the data path once:

160
00:12:40,788 --> 00:12:47,301
VOLUME /data in the Dockerfile documents it, and Compose files reference the same path.

161
00:12:47,867 --> 00:12:54,826
Prefer --mount (explicit keys, errors on missing bind sources) over -v in scripts.

162
00:12:56,567 --> 00:12:59,249
A PostgreSQL container ran for weeks.

163
00:12:59,249 --> 00:13:07,222
It was recreated for a configuration change, and the application now reports relation "orders" does not exist:

164
00:13:07,222 --> 00:13:09,251
the whole database is empty.

165
00:13:09,800 --> 00:13:09,962
1.

166
00:13:09,962 --> 00:13:12,950
Does the image declare a data volume?

167
00:13:13,500 --> 00:13:16,560
The database was run without a named volume.

168
00:13:16,560 --> 00:13:22,820
Recreating the container attached a fresh anonymous volume, so the database started empty.

169
00:13:24,567 --> 00:13:26,190
Recover the data first:

170
00:13:26,190 --> 00:13:32,401
copy the old anonymous volume into a named volume, then run the database with that name.

171
00:13:32,967 --> 00:13:40,260
Every stateful container gets a named volume (or a bind mount) for its data path:

172
00:13:40,260 --> 00:13:42,961
-v pgdata:/var/lib/postgresql.

173
00:13:43,500 --> 00:13:48,653
Read the image's VOLUME paths (and its documentation:

174
00:13:48,653 --> 00:13:54,195
PostgreSQL 18 images use /var/lib/postgresql, older ones.

175
00:13:55,933 --> 00:14:00,713
Someone added a .dockerignore to keep a notes file out of the image.

176
00:14:00,713 --> 00:14:04,016
Since then, the inventory image does not build:

177
00:14:04,016 --> 00:14:05,211
requirements.txt:

178
00:14:05,211 --> 00:14:09,288
not found, although the file is right there in the folder.

179
00:14:09,833 --> 00:14:10,678
1.

180
00:14:10,678 --> 00:14:27,998
Read the build output from the bottom up.

181
00:14:28,533 --> 00:14:35,994
.dockerignore contains *.txt, which excludes requirements.txt together with notes.txt.

182
00:14:35,994 --> 00:14:39,464
The Dockerfile's COPY requirements.txt .

183
00:14:39,464 --> 00:14:41,113
then finds nothing.

184
00:14:42,867 --> 00:14:50,786
Exclude exactly what should not be shipped (or use an exception, !requirements.txt, after the pattern):

185
00:14:51,333 --> 00:14:54,292
Review .dockerignore like code:

186
00:14:54,292 --> 00:14:59,733
broad patterns (.txt, .json, *) need explicit exceptions.

187
00:15:00,267 --> 00:15:04,815
An allow-list style is safest for small apps:

188
00:15:04,815 --> 00:15:09,666
* first, then !app.py, !requirements.txt, !src/.

189
00:15:11,400 --> 00:15:16,024
The pricing image downloads the latest exchange rates during the build.

190
00:15:16,024 --> 00:15:21,365
A new build was made this morning, yet the image still contains yesterday's rates.

191
00:15:21,900 --> 00:15:22,056
1.

192
00:15:22,056 --> 00:15:24,715
Did the build run the step at all?

193
00:15:25,267 --> 00:15:30,665
A build step that depends on something outside the build context (the network,

194
00:15:30,665 --> 00:15:33,572
the time, a remote repository) was cached:

195
00:15:33,572 --> 00:15:37,586
Docker cannot know that its result would be different now.

196
00:15:39,333 --> 00:15:42,195
Make the dependency part of the cache key.

197
00:15:42,195 --> 00:15:47,373
A build argument used by the step invalidates it whenever its value changes:

198
00:15:47,933 --> 00:15:50,846
Never rely on the cache for freshness:

199
00:15:50,846 --> 00:15:56,748
pin what you download (a version, a checksum) so the Dockerfile changes when.

200
00:15:57,300 --> 00:16:02,690
Rebuild regularly without cache, and with --pull to get the patched base image:

201
00:16:02,690 --> 00:16:04,055
a nightly or weekly.

202
00:16:05,800 --> 00:16:11,149
The Go API is a single small binary, but its image is hundreds of megabytes.

203
00:16:11,149 --> 00:16:15,302
Deployments are slow because every new server downloads it,

204
00:16:15,302 --> 00:16:20,230
and the security scanner reports findings in tools the API never uses.

205
00:16:20,767 --> 00:16:20,904
1.

206
00:16:20,904 --> 00:16:22,348
Which layers are big?

207
00:16:22,348 --> 00:16:28,056
docker history lists every layer with its size and the instruction that created it:

208
00:16:28,600 --> 00:16:33,120
The image was built in a single stage from the compiler image,

209
00:16:33,120 --> 00:16:40,775
so the build environment (compiler, sources, shell, package manager) ships to production with the binary.

210
00:16:42,533 --> 00:16:45,946
A multi-stage build (lessons 087, 089):

211
00:16:45,946 --> 00:16:54,348
compile in the golang stage, copy only the static binary into a minimal, non-root runtime image.

212
00:16:54,900 --> 00:17:05,425
Use multi-stage builds for every compiled language (Go, Java, Rust, .NET) and for front-end builds (Node.js →.

213
00:17:05,967 --> 00:17:09,093
Choose the smallest base that works:

214
00:17:09,093 --> 00:17:14,391
distroless, -alpine, -slim; avoid full OS images for runtime.

215
00:17:16,133 --> 00:17:21,816
The pricing image needs an access token to download a private price feed during the build.

216
00:17:21,816 --> 00:17:26,363
The token was passed with --build-arg, "so it is not in the Dockerfile".

217
00:17:26,900 --> 00:17:27,099
1.

218
00:17:27,099 --> 00:17:28,995
Where is it stored?

219
00:17:29,533 --> 00:17:36,793
The token was passed as a build argument and used by a RUN step, so BuildKit recorded it in the image history.

220
00:17:38,533 --> 00:17:40,426
First, revoke the token:

221
00:17:40,426 --> 00:17:46,656
it was published with the image, and removing the image does not un-publish it.

222
00:17:46,656 --> 00:17:50,993
Then pass the new token as a build secret (lesson 083):

223
00:17:51,533 --> 00:17:55,448
Never pass secrets with ARG, ENV or COPY.

224
00:17:56,000 --> 00:18:04,240
Scan images for secrets in CI and review docker history --no-trunc of release images (lesson 137).

225
00:18:06,000 --> 00:18:09,502
A penetration test of the opening-hours website reports:

226
00:18:09,502 --> 00:18:14,879
"a vulnerability in the web server gives an attacker a shell, and that shell is root".

227
00:18:14,879 --> 00:18:21,195
Nothing is broken functionally; the finding is that a compromise would be total inside the container.

228
00:18:21,733 --> 00:18:21,910
1.

229
00:18:21,910 --> 00:18:24,378
Who runs the server process?

230
00:18:24,933 --> 00:18:31,108
The Dockerfile has no USER instruction, so the server runs as root, the default.

231
00:18:32,867 --> 00:18:40,486
Create an unprivileged user and switch to it; keep the content owned by root so the server can read it but not change it:

232
00:18:41,033 --> 00:18:50,143
Every image ends with USER (a numeric uid works everywhere, including Kubernetes runAsNonRoot, lesson 081).

233
00:18:50,700 --> 00:18:56,650
Application files owned by root and read-only for the app; only data paths writable.

234
00:18:58,400 --> 00:19:03,794
The menu API answers every request, but Docker reports it as unhealthy.

235
00:19:03,794 --> 00:19:07,897
In Compose, the services that wait for it (depends_on:

236
00:19:07,897 --> 00:19:08,656
condition:

237
00:19:08,656 --> 00:19:10,860
service_healthy) never start.

238
00:19:11,400 --> 00:19:11,559
1.

239
00:19:11,559 --> 00:19:13,945
What does the healthcheck run?

240
00:19:14,500 --> 00:19:18,990
The HEALTHCHECK uses curl, which is not installed in the image.

241
00:19:20,733 --> 00:19:25,103
Check with a tool the image really contains (here Python itself),

242
00:19:25,103 --> 00:19:28,263
rather than installing curl only for the check:

243
00:19:28,800 --> 00:19:32,718
Test the healthcheck command in the image when you write it:

244
00:19:32,718 --> 00:19:35,070
docker run --rm IMAGE sh -c 'CHECK'.

245
00:19:35,633 --> 00:19:37,567
Use what the image has:

246
00:19:37,567 --> 00:19:44,378
wget in Alpine/BusyBox, the language runtime in slim images, a small healthcheck.

247
00:19:46,133 --> 00:19:52,070
The nightly report job got a memory limit of 64 MB during a cost review.

248
00:19:52,070 --> 00:19:58,913
Since then it dies without a single error message, every night, with exit code 137.

249
00:19:59,467 --> 00:19:59,656
1.

250
00:19:59,656 --> 00:20:01,741
Who ended the process?

251
00:20:02,300 --> 00:20:08,209
The job needs more than 150 MB at its peak; the new limit is 64 MB.

252
00:20:08,209 --> 00:20:12,795
The kernel kills it as soon as it crosses the limit.

253
00:20:14,533 --> 00:20:19,906
Set the limit from the measured peak plus headroom (or make the job use less memory,

254
00:20:19,906 --> 00:20:24,063
for example by streaming the data instead of loading it at once):

255
00:20:24,600 --> 00:20:26,775
Measure before you limit:

256
00:20:26,775 --> 00:20:33,910
docker stats under realistic load, the cgroup's memory.peak for jobs (lesson 098).

257
00:20:34,467 --> 00:20:43,021
Alert on oom events and on exit code 137 in your monitoring; a restart policy alone only hides the kills.

258
00:20:44,767 --> 00:20:52,151
The thumbnail service became several times slower after its deployment configuration got --cpus 0.25.

259
00:20:52,151 --> 00:20:58,146
There are no errors and the server has idle CPU cores, but every job takes longer.

260
00:20:58,700 --> 00:20:58,881
1.

261
00:20:58,881 --> 00:21:01,235
Which limit is configured?

262
00:21:01,800 --> 00:21:07,340
The limit of a quarter of a CPU is far below what the job needs to finish in time.

263
00:21:09,100 --> 00:21:15,259
Limits can be changed on a running container with docker update (Compose:

264
00:21:15,259 --> 00:21:17,790
deploy.resources.limits.cpus):

265
00:21:18,333 --> 00:21:24,167
Size CPU limits from measurements under realistic load (docker stats,

266
00:21:24,167 --> 00:21:27,718
cpu.stat), and recheck after code changes.

267
00:21:28,267 --> 00:21:30,834
Watch throttling, not only usage:

268
00:21:30,834 --> 00:21:37,136
throttled_usec growing is the signal; monitoring systems export it per container.

269
00:21:38,900 --> 00:21:42,327
The team's private registry now requires a login.

270
00:21:42,327 --> 00:21:50,000
The release script pushes as before and fails with no basic auth credentials; a colleague logs in and gets 401 Unauthorized.

271
00:21:51,533 --> 00:21:51,701
1.

272
00:21:51,701 --> 00:21:54,293
What does the registry require?

273
00:21:54,833 --> 00:21:59,765
The client had no credentials for localhost:5000 (no docker login),

274
00:21:59,765 --> 00:22:03,298
and the colleague's login used a wrong password.

275
00:22:05,033 --> 00:22:13,218
Log in with the right credentials, read from standard input so the password never appears in the shell history or the process list:

276
00:22:13,767 --> 00:22:21,143
In CI, log in with a dedicated token from the secret store, never a personal password:

277
00:22:21,143 --> 00:22:23,201
docker/login-action, or.

278
00:22:23,767 --> 00:22:31,166
Use tokens with the narrowest scope (pull-only for servers, push for CI) and an expiry date.

279
00:22:32,900 --> 00:22:35,081
Three reports in one morning:

280
00:22:35,081 --> 00:22:39,367
a new server cannot start the proxy (pull access denied),

281
00:22:39,367 --> 00:22:42,977
a CI job cannot find its base image (not found),

282
00:22:42,977 --> 00:22:51,475
and a training room full of laptops gets toomanyrequests / 429 Too Many Requests for images that certainly exist.

283
00:22:52,033 --> 00:22:52,207
1.

284
00:22:52,207 --> 00:22:54,468
Is the image already here?

285
00:22:55,033 --> 00:22:55,283
1.

286
00:22:55,283 --> 00:22:59,025
A typo in the repository name:

287
00:22:59,025 --> 00:22:59,773
ngnix.

288
00:22:59,773 --> 00:23:00,023
2.

289
00:23:01,767 --> 00:23:02,244
Typos:

290
00:23:02,244 --> 00:23:08,453
use the exact reference from the registry's tag list, and pin it (lesson 020).

291
00:23:08,453 --> 00:23:14,741
The correct image is already local, so it runs without contacting the registry:

292
00:23:15,300 --> 00:23:25,280
Pin exact image references (nginx:1.30-alpine, or a digest) and copy them from the registry, not from memory.

293
00:23:25,833 --> 00:23:31,433
Log in to Docker Hub in CI and on servers, with a read-only access token.
